PWM CONTROL - GP18 BUZZER AND GP19 SERVO

On this teaching setup, GP18 connects to a buzzer (channel A) and
GP19 connects to a servo motor (channel B). Both use PWM slice 1
and share ONE frequency; changing it affects both devices.

READ FIRST
  pins
  pwmctl status
  help pwmctl

COMMANDS (from userland/apps/pwmctl.c)
  pwmctl freq HZ        shared frequency, 8..100000 Hz
  pwmctl a DUTY         GP18 buzzer duty, 0..100 percent
  pwmctl b DUTY         GP19 servo duty, 0..100 percent
  pwmctl off            stop both channels' duty
  pwmctl status         report current configuration
Duty supports fractional tenths of percent per CLI parser.

READ-ONLY PROCEDURE
  1) confirm the buzzer on GP18 and servo on GP19;
  2) run pins and pwmctl status; record frequency and both duties;
  3) determine servo period, allowable pulse widths and travel range;
  4) request authorization before ANY frequency/duty change;
  5) verify command result AND externally observed actuator state.
  Note: pwmctl off sets BOTH duties to zero, not just one.

PITFALLS
  There is no independent frequency per channel in slice 1.
  A 523 Hz audible buzzer tone is NOT a safe servo configuration
  while GP19 is attached. Servo pulses need a compatible period
  and width; never assume generic duty values are safe.
  'pwmctl a 100' is not a harmless diagnostic command.
  Changing PWM can affect other currently running demos.
  Use the /dev/pwm/1 virtual files only after reading driver
  documentation; prefer the tested pwmctl CLI for beginners.
